{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310794,"nonce":"r3FSmAEsCGyhMTO0fi9Srx6RN70=","title":"Phase-4 Q4_K_M qmatmul in pure Haxe is CORRECT (bit-exact). The '28x slower than Rust matmulXTQ' was a DEBUG-MODE ARTIFACT — frame-tracking ...","message":"The Phase-4 throughput benchmark (the meaningful microbench, /tmp/q4chk/bench.hx). Q4_K_M weight, x=[1,K], Sys.time() wall-clock, T=30, pkill hygiene, checksum to defeat DCE. The Haxe qmatmul is bit-exact (test_q4km_qmatmul) — correctness is NOT the issue.\n\n- NATIVE rows=1024 K=2048: rust **0.12ms**, Haxe dot **5.18ms**, Haxe dot-NO-header **3.44ms** → ~28x. Force-JIT (`--tier-start-interpreted false`) gives the SAME → NOT interpreter-bound.\n- NATIVE rows=2048 K=2048: rust **0.24ms** vs Haxe **10.3ms** (~44x).\n- WASM rows=1024 K=2048: \"rust\" 0.15ms dispatches to the HOST native matmul (NOT apples-to-apples); the real wasm A/B is in-guest-Haxe vs in-guest-Rust (not yet isolated).\n\nInstrumented the merged-module InliningPass in `compile_helpers.rs` (`compile_haxe_to_mir_with_defines_and_cache`, confirmed = the native `run` path). All 38 SIMD wrapper bodies are present + tiny; un-gating the inliner for native ran it (`modified=true, functions_inlined: 115`); **bench timing did NOT move** (3.46-\u003e3.54ms = noise). Reverted; compile_helpers back to wasm-gated. Inlining alone is not the lever — which itself proves the per-block SCALAR work dominates, not the wrapper calls.\n\nI claimed native lowers VectorDot to an ~11-instr swiden/imul/iadd_pairwise widen sequence (cranelift_backend.rs:4678) instead of sdot. FALSE — I confused rayzor's CLIF *emission* with the final isel output. CLIF has NO dot opcode, so the widen tree is the ONLY way to express an i8 dot; cranelift's aarch64 backend then FOLDS it. Ground truth (workflow wf_fdf9d9b6-949): a standalone crate pinned to the EXACT cranelift (codegen 0.134.0, git rev 8cb28bc, = wasmtime 47, via root Cargo.toml `[patch.crates-io]`) emitting the byte-identical CLIF tree from cranelift_backend.rs:4688-4698, compiled via the same `cranelift_native::builder()` (cranelift_backend.rs:279), produced **`sdot v0.4s, v0.4s, v1.4s, v2.4s`** (sdot=1) on FEAT_DotProd host; control with has_dotprod=false gave smull/smull2/addp/saddlp/add. The rule = `lower.isle:368-377` (rule prio 8, gated ONLY on `(use_dotprod)`) — a GENERAL structural CLIF peephole, NOT wasm-frontend/relaxed_dot-specific; ANY producer of that tree (wasm frontend code_translator.rs:2605 OR rayzor's native cranelift_backend) hits it. `use_dotprod` (isle.rs:182) = isa has_dotprod, auto-enabled by cranelift_native (native/src/lib.rs:119 is_aarch64_feature_detected!(\"dotprod\")); M-series has FEAT_DotProd=1. So native JIT == wasm == NEON on the dot. darmie's relaxed_dot-\u003eSDOT PR DOES cover native. (The `version=\"0.134\"` in compiler/Cargo.toml is NOT crates.io — root `[patch.crates-io]` Cargo.toml:75-82 pins all cranelift-* to git rev 8cb28bc, same rev as wasmtime 47; Cargo.lock confirms a single cranelift-codegen 0.134.0 from that git rev.)\n\nPer-block SCALAR machinery around the (already-SDOT) dot in the pure-Haxe kernel: Q4_K_M f16 scale decode (pow2 LOOP, ~34% of Haxe time as header), bsums/d/dmin reconstruction, 6-bit scale unpack, super-block bookkeeping. Rust matmulXTQ does this in tight code; the Haxe version does it naively. The dot is NOT the bottleneck on any target.\n\nThe header decode (f16 d/dmin + 6-bit sub-scales, ~2/3 of the per-token kernel) is amortizable: weights are static across all decode tokens, so decode every block's scales ONCE at load into side arrays (dsc[blk*8+s]=d*sc6, dmn=dmin*mn6) and the per-token matvec reads pre-decoded f32 scales. Implemented in /tmp/q4chk/bench.hx (predecodeScales/q4DotPre/haxeDotPre). NATIVE --release best-config result: haxeDot 1.11ms -\u003e **haxeDotPre 0.37ms** (lands EXACTLY on the dot-only floor haxeDotNoHdr 0.37ms), gap to Rust **9.2x -\u003e 3.1x**, BIT-EXACT (pc==hc==nc=981013191.0142387). One-time predecode 0.95ms, pays back in ~1.3 tokens. The decode-path header cost is fully eliminated.\n\nIn-guest wasm (RAYZOR_WASM_HOST_MATMUL=0, --release): Haxe dot floor (haxeDotNoHdr) = **13.8ms = 37x native (0.37ms) and 94x the in-guest Rust SIMD128 kernel (0.147ms)**. Pre-decode helps only marginally on wasm (16.6-\u003e15.4ms; header is a small fraction of the large wasm dot). KEY: the wasm path does NOT get frame-tracking (instrument_modules_for_stack_traces is tiered-backend-ONLY, native-only) so 13.8ms is NOT debug-contaminated; AND adding the O2 PassManager to the wasm MIR (wasm_cmd.rs, tested+REVERTED) does NOTHING because the pipeline is rayzor-MIR -\u003e wasm bytecode -\u003e WASMTIME's own Cranelift, which re-optimizes (MIR-O2 redundant on wasm, unlike native where rayzor-MIR feeds Cranelift directly). The Rust kernel has a ~1.2x wasm penalty (LLVM-\u003ewasm near-native); the Haxe kernel has a ~37x wasm penalty -\u003e the gap is rayzor's **wasm_backend SIMD codegen quality** for the Haxe kernel vs LLVM's for the Rust kernel. STRATEGIC: Haxifying the quant matmul would REGRESS wasm decode ~94x (in-guest Rust \u003e\u003e in-guest Haxe) — the FFI-fusion-barrier premise is wrong on wasm. KEEP the dot as Rust FFI on wasm; the real wasm Haxe lever is fixing wasm_backend SIMD lowering (is SDOT firing? are loads/nibble/sum efficient? stack-machine overhead?), a separate deep investigation. Native Haxe kernel IS viable (3.1x); wasm Haxe kernel is not (37-94x).\n\nNOT the dot (relaxed_dot-\u003eSDOT fires under embedded wasmtime 47), NOT wrapper inlining (wrappers ARE inlined now — the old \"non-inlined SIMD wrappers\" note + the wasm-A/B agent's claim are STALE/REFUTED). The real cause: **rayzor's wasm_backend lowers reducible Haxe loops into a 34-arm `br_table` state machine** keyed on a state-local (local 74) — a relooper that puts EACH SIMD op in its own basic block. So all 18 v128 values spill/reload across the dispatch boundary and wasmtime's regalloc can't keep them in registers. subDot: 70 locals (18 v128 + 52 i32) for a ~6-v128-op kernel, 189 local.get/set, per-op pattern `\u003cop\u003e; local.set N; local.get N; local.set M(redundant SSA copy); local.set 74(state); br` + 12x `call 867` no-op frame/trap stubs in the hot path. Native cwasm disasm: subDot = **621 aarch64 instrs, 309 load/store, 8 SIMD-compute ops, spill:compute ~38:1**; the single sdot is bracketed by 3 ldur q,[sp] + stur q,[sp] + branch back to dispatcher. RUST ref (vec_dot_q4_K_q8_K, func 231) = ONE flat superblock, 16 back-to-back relaxed_dots in-register, 10 v128 locals NEVER spilled, 0 v128.store, 0 in-body calls, deferred hsum. So same wasmtime/cranelift rev gives Rust near-native (1.2x) on identical opcodes -\u003e NOT a Cranelift-vs-LLVM floor, a wasm_backend STRUCTURING deficiency. FIX (wasm_backend, the L-wasm lever): (1) emit reducible loops as a single structured `loop`/`block` with straight-line body (not a br_table-on-state-local dispatcher) so SIMD producer-\u003econsumer dataflow stays on the operand stack — collapses the 18 v128 locals + 38:1 spill; (2) drop the redundant `local.set N; local.get N; local.set M` copy pattern; (3) strip inline `call 867` no-op frame/trap stubs from leaf SIMD kernels. This is THE wasm lever — closing it could take the Haxe wasm kernel from 37x toward the Rust 1.2x, making Haxe-on-wasm viable (currently Haxifying the matmul would regress wasm decode ~94x). Workflow wf_abed77b5-63c.\n\nImplemented in compiler/src/codegen/wasm_backend.rs (+780/-1, strictly additive): try_structured_shape probe (reuses DominatorTree/LoopNestInfo) + build_structured_loop emitter that emits `(block $exit (loop $hdr \u003cbody straight-line\u003e \u003cback-edge phi-set\u003e (br back)))` for a single reducible non-nested counted loop, incl. recursive structured if/else for the in-loop diamond (subDot's isHi ternary — the plan's \"reject diamonds\" was wrong, subDot HAS one). Falls through to the UNCHANGED br_table path otherwise; escape hatch RAYZOR_WASM_NO_STRUCTURED=1. VERIFIED (independently re-run): BIT-EXACT (native hc=pc=nc=981013191.0142387 unchanged, wasm=995233973.469957 identical structured ON and OFF), subDot WAT br_table 34-arm-state-machine -\u003e 0 (one real loop+block, relaxed_dot in flat body), wasm haxeDotNoHdr 13.74-\u003e10.36ms (-24.6%), haxeDot 16.75-\u003e13.71 (-18%), 16-test A/B byte-identical, native unaffected. PARTIAL: gap 36x-\u003e27x native, NOT to 1.2x — subDot still 72 local.set/82 local.get (the per-value-local round-trip model in emit_block_instructions kept verbatim). REMAINING LEVERS in order: (1) operand-stack temps (keep single-use SIMD values on the wasm stack, not local.set/get each) — the big one; (2) nested-loop structuring (hot haxeDot* band loops still br_table, rejected loops.len()\u003e1). Implementing agent transcript in session. THREADING (checked): matmulXTQ engages 6-worker pool but bandwidth-bound (workers=1==default at bench size, ~2 eff cores on real decode per quant.rs:1022); Haxe kernels are SINGLE-THREADED (1 core, 99.9% CPU) — so the Haxe-vs-Rust gap is codegen NOT thread-count. Future: parallelize Haxe kernel via WorkerPool.parallelRows once per-core codegen is competitive.\n\nThe wasm_backend materializes every SSA value into a local (subDot: 72 local.set/82 local.get) and wasmtime does NOT coalesce them. Lever 1 = \"operand-stack temps\" = run binaryen `wasm-opt -O3 -all` on the linked wasm (simplify-locals/coalesce-locals): subDot 237 lines/72 local.set -\u003e 55 lines/3 local.set, relaxed_dot preserved. COMMITTED dc73ae25 (after structured loops cbbe4266) via the `wasm-opt = \"0.116\"` binaryen Rust crate (bundled, NO external binary), DEFAULT-ON for both run --wasm + build --wasm (maybe_wasm_opt in src/wasm_cmd.rs); escape RAYZOR_WASM_NO_OPT=1; errors fall back to unoptimized. First build compiles binaryen (~3min one-time). 2x2 A/B (all bit-exact hc=pc=nc=995233973.469957): br_table+noopt 13.74ms, structured-only 10.61, br_table+opt 8.43, **structured+opt 3.52ms**. The two levers are COMPLEMENTARY (wasm-opt can't undo the br_table dispatch; structured loops give it a clean straight-line body). Combined: wasm Q4 dot 13.74-\u003e3.52ms = 3.9x, gap to native 37x-\u003e9.3x, gap to in-guest Rust 94x-\u003e24x. Don't hand-roll a stackifier — binaryen does it + more (270-\u003e186KB whole module). PRODUCTIONIZE: prefer the `wasm-opt`/binaryen Rust crate (bundled, no external dep, but heavy C++ build) over shell-out; default-on for `build --wasm` (AOT), worth the ~1-2s pass for `run --wasm` given 3x. Decode-path pre-decode (native 3x, haxeDot 1.11-\u003e0.37) is separate + also landed in the bench.\n\nRe-verified the \"wrappers inline\" claim directly: `rayzor build \u003ckernel\u003e.hx --target wasm` dumps the post-inliner user module to /tmp/rayzor_prelink.wasm; `wasm-tools print` it. test_q4km_dot.hx = **0 `call $SIMD*` wrapper calls**, $Main.subDot body is pure inline v128 (v128.load/and, i8x16.shr_u, i32x4.relaxed_dot_i8x16_i7x16_add_s = SDOT). test_q4km_qmatmul.hx = only **4 surviving wrapper calls, ALL `SIMD16i8_extract`**, all in $Main.q4DotPtr's reduction tail (cold, O(rows) not O(blocks)). Root: SIMD16i8_extract is a 264-line runtime-lane SELECT-CHAIN (16 lanes) \u003e the inliner's 50-instr cost threshold (inlining.rs:179), so the cost model correctly declines it; the tiny hot ops are all well under. 38 wrapper DEFS remain as dead code (binaryen wasm-opt DCEs them, 166→77KB). FIX for the residual (optional, cold): rewrite the kernel hsum to use SIMD4i32.sum()/vector_reduce (→addv) or const-lane extracts instead of runtime-lane `.get(lane)` — the \"defer hsum to vector domain\" lever ([[perf_wasm_v8_vs_wasmtime_2x]]); NOT a compiler-inlining change. Confirmed the real wasm compile path (wasm_cmd.rs cmd_run_wasm:344 / cmd_build_wasm:435) always passes the \"wasm\" define (wasm_defines:141) → the post-merge inliner DOES fire in production. CAVEAT to check for FULL cross-module nue: the post-merge inliner runs only on mir_modules.last() (compile_helpers.rs:153) — verify nue's hot kernel lands in that module (single-file tests trivially do).\n\nRan /tmp/q4bench.hx in-guest wasm (RAYZOR_WASM_HOST_MATMUL=0): Rust in-guest 0.281ms (≈1.2x native, fine); Haxe naive 7.85ms/28x, predecode 6.49ms/23x, deferred 7.14ms/25x, dotonly 11.65ms/41x. Gap is real + large.\n**The \"br_table nested-loop structuring is THE wasm lever\" theory is WRONG for the hot path** (disasm of the actual inlined hot dot fn, func 279, 528 lines, POST wasm-opt): br_table=0, loops=0 — it is FULLY UNROLLED, 8 relaxed_dots fire (SDOT confirmed on wasm), but local.get=120 / local.set=15 for 8 dots. The 187 br_table in the module are in COLD outer orchestration loops (amortized over the inner unrolled dot), NOT the hot work. An isolation bench (single-loop vs 2-level-nested, same SIMD work) gave single==nested on wasm (both 21.5ms) → confirms loop structuring is NOT the dominant cost. (Native single() got DCE'd so that bench was partly flawed, but the wasm single==nested result stands.)\n**Real lever = operand-stack temps**: wasm_backend materializes every SSA value into a local (120 local.get/8 dots); wasm-opt DID run (386→231KB) but only partially coalesces → v128 values spill instead of staying in registers; wasmtime cranelift can't keep them in regs. Fix = emit single-use v128 values on the wasm operand stack instead of local.set/get each. DEEP wasm_backend change, UNCERTAIN upside (wasm has NO LLVM — only wasmtime cranelift, a higher floor than native; the LLVM SDOT win does NOT transfer to wasm). \nNote: the LLVM partial.reduce→SDOT fix (native, merged 36173918) is irrelevant to wasm — wasm already uses relaxed_dot→SDOT via the wasm_backend VectorDot lowering + wasmtime cranelift.\n\nQ: is wasmtime run optimized? YES — wasm_runner.rs:1845-1846 Strategy::Cranelift + cranelift_opt_level(OptLevel::Speed) (cranelift's top perf level; no O3 equiv), wasmtime release-compiled in target/release/rayzor. So the 23-41x is NOT a debug artifact.\nQ: fixable upstream wasmtime? NO — the Rust in-guest kernel is the control: Rust→LLVM→wasm (clean operand-stack wasm) runs through the SAME embedded wasmtime at 0.281ms (≈1.2x native). Haxe runs through the IDENTICAL wasmtime at 6.5ms. Same JIT, same opcodes, same SDOT — only variable is the wasm rayzor EMITS. wasmtime/cranelift already handles clean v128 wasm near-native; the gap is rayzor's wasm_backend emitting local-heavy wasm (120 local.get/8 dots). =\u003e fix is RAYZOR-SIDE (operand-stack temps in wasm_backend), NOT upstream. **The prize is now PROVEN reachable**: ≈0.28ms (Rust in-guest) is the ceiling for clean Haxe-emitted wasm → ~1.2x native instead of 23x, if rayzor emits operand-stack form like LLVM. This makes the operand-stack lever clearly worth it (proven upside, rayzor-only, not blocked on upstream).\n\nWalked back the local-heavy claim: external wasm-opt on PATH DID run -O3 -all (wasm_cmd.rs:262 passes the flags via the legacy fallback; rayzor-wasm-opt helper is NOT built but the fallback covers it). Post-opt the hot dot has 15 local.set (coalesced fine) + 120 local.get = MULTI-USE reads of 15 values (normal, not pathological). So locals are NOT the smoking gun. What IS true: same wasmtime, Rust in-guest 0.281ms vs Haxe 6.5ms = ~25x per-block, ~10x even for an isolation pure-SIMD dot (wasmstruct.hx, though that bench was DCE-flawed on native). The exact dominant factor in rayzor's emitted Haxe wasm is NOT yet pinned — candidates: per-block scalar glue (f64 scale math vs Rust f32, Array\u003cFloat\u003e bounds-checked reads, nibble unpack inline), or a per-SDOT instruction-count overhead vs the Rust dot. NEXT (proper): side-by-side disasm of the Haxe dot loop (func 279) vs the Rust vec_dot in the SAME linked wasm, counting wasm instrs executed per relaxed_dot. Lesson: I over-narrowed to br_table then to operand-stack from memory; both refuted by measurement. wasm is a harder frontier (no LLVM) — pin the bottleneck by disasm before any rewrite.\n\nProfiled Haxe dot (func 279) vs deferred (func 281) vs Rust (func ~1024) in /tmp/q4w.wat. The static profile FINGERED a per-sub-block hsum scalarization chain (func 279: 32 extract_lane + 24 f32.convert + 33 f32.mul + 16 f32.add per block; Rust loop body has ZERO scalar FP / ZERO extract). BUT measurement REFUTES it as the cause: the deferred kernel (func 281) compiled to vector-domain = **4 extract_lane / 2 convert / 0 f32.add for 16 dots** (≈90% of the chain GONE), yet measured 7.14ms — NOT faster than predecode 6.49ms, both ~23-25x Rust. So removing the scalarization chain barely helps → it is ~10% of the cost, NOT the 25x driver. (4th refuted theory: br_table, operand-stack-count, hsum-scalarization all knocked down by measurement.)\n**Real conclusion**: instruction COUNT is only ~1.5x more per dot (46 vs 31), but TIME is 25x → the gap is per-INSTRUCTION execution cost of the CORE SIMD path (load + nibble and/ushr + relaxed_dot + vector accumulate) — the SAME ops Rust runs, ~10x slower in rayzor-emitted wasm on the SAME wasmtime. Prime suspect = v128 values routed through wasm LOCALS (local_shuffle ~15-17/dot, similar COUNT to Rust but rayzor's are v128 → wasmtime allocates stack slots / SPILLS 16-byte vectors, vs Rust/LLVM keeping v128 in registers). This is a cranelift-codegen-quality floor on rayzor's wasm SHAPE, not a single fixable pattern. DEFINITIVE next step to confirm = dump the JITted aarch64 (cwasm disasm) and count v128 ldr/str q spills in the Haxe dot vs Rust dot (per [[perf_wasm_v8_vs_wasmtime_2x]] which earlier found subDot=621 aarch64 instrs, 309 load/store, spill:compute 38:1). Wasm is a deep frontier (no LLVM); native LLVM path (1.44x, merged 36173918) is where Haxe is competitive.\n\nAdded a cwasm dump hook to wasm_runner.rs (RAYZOR_WASM_CWASM_DUMP=path → engine.precompile_module → ELF aarch64, objdump with llvm-objdump). Uses rayzor's EXACT embedded wasmtime (5937fbc). Clean bare-SDOT-loop bench /tmp/flatdot.hx (single loop, data-dependent acc, no Q4 machinery): NATIVE-LLVM 2.48 ns/sdot vs WASM 20.5 ns/sdot = **8.3x floor on even a trivial SDOT loop**. \nDisasm of the flatdot wasm loop (cwasm function[880]): the accumulator stays in v16 REGISTER-RESIDENT — NO v128 spill (str_q=0). The 8.3x is per-iteration LOOP OVERHEAD that LLVM removes for native but cranelift leaves in for rayzor's wasm:\n  (1) `ldr x0,[x2,#0x30]; ldr x0,[x0]` EVERY iteration = reloads the wasm LINEAR-MEMORY base from vmctx, NOT hoisted (despite memory_may_move(false) which should let it cache the base for the whole fn).\n  (2) address recomputed each iter (and/mov/lsl/add) — NOT strength-reduced to a pointer increment.\n  (3) 2-branch header-test loop (back-edge `b` → header `cmp`+`b.gt`) — not loop-rotated to a single bottom-test.\nThese are LICM + strength-reduction + loop-rotation. The Rust in-guest kernel (LLVM→wasm, SAME wasmtime) = 0.281ms PROVES cranelift CAN optimize clean wasm — so it is the SHAPE rayzor emits, fixable RAYZOR-SIDE. Proven ceiling.\nALL OTHER THEORIES REFUTED BY MEASUREMENT/DISASM: br_table-in-hot-dot (unrolled), operand-stack-local-count (wasm-opt coalesced to 15), hsum-scalarization (deferred kernel removed 90%, no speedup), v128-spills (acc register-resident), per-sdot-machine-quality (Haxe 41 instr/sdot ≈ Rust 41). The Q4 matmul adds ~2x more (scalar per-block machinery) on top of the 8.3x bare-loop floor → ~16-25x.\nFIX (rayzor wasm_backend, the real lever): emit loops cranelift can optimize — single bottom-test loop (rotation), strength-reduce the index→pointer, and structure so cranelift hoists the linear-memory base. NEXT: find why cranelift won't hoist the base in rayzor's loop vs Rust's (loop-carried dep? the structured-loop shape? a memory-access pattern that aliases?), then fix the emission.\n\nIsolation (bare SDOT loop, /tmp/multiacc.hx): single-acc (latency-bound) vs 4 independent accumulators → NATIVE 2.39→0.47 ns/dot (5.1x), WASM 25.4→4.60 ns/dot (5.5x). LLVM does NOT auto-break the int-reduction chain, so manual multi-acc helps both. The Haxe kernels (subDot/q4Dot) were SINGLE-accumulator = latency-bound; on wasm the per-iteration overhead can't overlap the sdot latency → serializes.\nApplied to the REAL Q4 kernel (/tmp/q4bench.hx q4DotMA4, 8 subDotVec into 4 accumulators): **NATIVE 1.46x→1.12x Rust (near parity!)** but **WASM 25.5x→21.6x (only ~6-15%)**. Decisive: predecode/deferred/MA4 are ALL ~6-7ms on wasm regardless of dot handling → the dot is NOT the wasm bottleneck; the per-block SCALAR machinery is (bounds-checked Array\u003cFloat\u003e/Array\u003cInt\u003e scale+bsum reads, the imin loop, f32 scale math, Bytes.getDouble), which cranelift (no LLVM on wasm) compiles ~10x worse than LLVM. dot-only is even SLOWER (41x) due to its triple-nested loop (br_table orchestration) — loop structure confound.\n**ACTIONABLE NATIVE WIN**: apply multi-accumulator (≥4 independent SIMD4i32 accumulators across the 8 sub-blocks) to nue's real Q4 kernels → native Haxe ≈ 1.12x Rust = at-parity, combined with the merged SDOT fix. Kernel-source change, proven.\n**WASM path** (harder, cranelift-scalar floor): minimize the per-block scalar machinery — use Ptr (no Array bounds checks) for the scale/bsum arrays, vectorize the scale application (SIMD4f instead of scalar f32 mul/add), cut per-block ops. Would help but against a cranelift-vs-LLVM scalar-codegen floor (no LLVM on wasm). The dot itself is solved (SDOT + multi-acc).\n\nThe 85% wasm scalar machinery was NOT array bounds checks (Array\u003cFloat\u003e[] = 6.5ns/read on wasm, fine). It was **Bytes.getDouble = 300ns/read** — a 46x anomaly. Root cause (wat): `(import \"rayzor\" \"haxe_bytes_get_double\")` — Bytes.getDouble/setDouble compile to a HOST-IMPORT CALL on wasm (wasm→host boundary crossing per read), NOT an inline guest f64.load. Micro-bench /tmp/arrread.hx: native array==bytes (0.93ns); wasm array 6.5ns vs **bytes 300ns**. The q4 kernel called dBytes.getDouble(b*8) once/block × 16384 = ~4.9ms = the entire 5.2ms \"scalar machinery\".\nKERNEL-SOURCE FIX (proven): replace Bytes.getDouble in hot loops with Array\u003cFloat\u003e. Combined with multi-accumulator, the full Q4 matmul:\n  **WASM: 21.7x → 3.44x Rust (6.3x speedup)** | **NATIVE: 1.12x → 1.08x (parity)**.\n  (dot-work floor on wasm = 0.825ms = 2.9x; the kernel is now near that floor.)\nSYSTEMIC COMPILER FIX (the real lever, affects ALL wasm Bytes code incl nue GGUF load): lower Bytes.getDouble/setDouble/getInt32/etc. to INLINE guest f64.load/store on wasm (Bytes IS guest-resident; address via Bytes.address()) — exactly like SIMD16i8.load is inlined to v128.load — instead of routing to the haxe_bytes_get_double host import. Where: wasm_backend / stdlib Bytes lowering; mirror the SIMD/Ptr inline-load intercept. This would make Bytes hot-path ~46x faster on wasm everywhere.\nSUMMARY of the full Haxe-vs-Rust story: NATIVE solved (parity, multi-acc+SDOT merged); WASM now 3.44x (multi-acc + avoid-Bytes.getDouble kernel-source) with the systemic Bytes-inline compiler fix as the next lever to push it toward the ~2.9x dot floor and below.\n\nMade Bytes scalar accessors inline guest loads/stores on wasm instead of host-import calls. compiler/src/codegen/wasm_backend.rs: new `try_inline_bytes_accessor` (called at the top of the CallDirect arm in emit_instruction; early-returns if handled). Emits `\u003ctype\u003e.load( i32.load(handle, offset=0) + pos )` — HaxeBytes.ptr is at offset 0 (runtime-wasm/src/tensor.rs:54), handle is *HaxeBytes, wasm ptrs are i32. Covers getDouble(F64Load)/getInt32(I32Load)/getFloat(F32Load+F64Promote) + setDouble/setInt32/setFloat(F32Demote). SAFETY: only Bytes-UNIQUE typed names matched (getDouble etc.); deliberately NOT bare get/set — \"_get\"/\"_set\" suffix also matches haxe_array_get/stringmap_get → would corrupt non-Bytes calls (caught + removed before trusting any b